       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 1000 mg of sertraline daily is about five times the maximum labeled dose. I know of no indication or protocol that supports it, and it's in the range where toxicity is expected. Entering it would put the patient at serious risk, and I can't treat the order as a routine one.\n\nThis isn't a judgment about your clinical skill. It's that I have no way to confirm the order is safe, and an error here could cause serious harm.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** I can do 50, 100, or 200 mg by mouth once daily right now.\n- **Talk to pharmacy or psychiatry first.** If you think the patient needs more than 200 mg/day, they can review it with you. They can also tell you about a dose-escalation plan or an alternative agent.\n- **Place the order through your hospital's override process.** If a high-dose override exists, it would include pharmacist verification and the documented rationale. I'd rather not bypass that step.\n\nIf the order was meant to be a different dose or drug, tell me and I'll enter it."
